  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ANGAU Hospital Redevelopment Project contributed to strengthen infectious disease management. Through the Clinical Support Program, the project deployed clinicians and infectious disease experts to help improve the management of patients with tuberculosis (TB), and other infectious diseases. A total of 66 staff undertook training including in the following areas: hands-on Drug Resistant-TB Clinical Training, Hands-on TB Counselling and Education Training, Infection Prevention and Control Training. Experts worked with the PNG National TB Program and hospital staff to ensure that PNG-approved treatment regimes were being followed by hospital staff for the management and treatment of drug resistant TB in the hospital&#x2019;s outpatients and inpatient units. The trainings supported hospital staff to better manage infection prevention and control processes and systems within the new redeveloped facilities and across the whole hospital.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ANGAU Hospital Wide Database (AHWD) was developed as a new functionality, allowing collation of clinical information across hospital services and data entry into the electronic National Health Information System. Compared to the previous predominately paper-based system, the AHWD provides a more efficient system to collect clinical indicators and helps link data to decision-making to resource allocation within the PNG health system. During the 2023 budget preparation process, the AHWD provided clinical indicators for a performance framework that supported a successful budget submission.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Clinical Support Program deployed Australian clinicians and infectious disease experts to ANGAU Hospital to help improve the management of patients with COVID-19, tuberculosis and other infectious diseases. The peer clinician team was imbedded with intensive care and emergency department staff, working to streamline processes and structure systems (e.g. triage and screening practices) to better manage infectious disease outbreaks. This helped ensure that there is more efficient use of personnel, especially during infectious disease outbreaks, whilst continually training staff on relevant patient assessment skills and usage of new clinical and personal protective equipment.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project conducted an Accessibility Audit in late 2023 led by people with disabilities (after delays of this activity in 2022). The Audit provided an opportunity to identify further areas for physical improvement and to increase awareness of the work done to date in improving accessibility, so that more community members would be encouraged to seek services over time. The Audit provided recommendations and, those within project scope, were ad-dressed as part of the project&#x2019;s Minor Works program. Although the initial timeline of this activity was delayed, it was important to complete this before the end of the project as an opportunity to improve the capacity of the infrastructure to provide accessible health services to people living with a disability. It is understood that this type of Audit had not been conducted across other infrastructure projects in PNG to date.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
